      "id": 1,
      "text": "Palmer was incorporated in 1925 and renamed for Clarence William Palmer, a landowner who owned much of the town's original land.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palmer%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town thrived in the early 20th century thanks to mining operations by the Tennessee Consolidated Coal Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palmer%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Grundy County Miners Museum in Palmer stands as a solemn tribute to the coal miners who once worked beneath the Cumberland Plateau.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.southcumberlandchamber.com/palmer-tennessee-south-cumberland-chamber-of-commerce"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Palmer sits atop the rugged Cumberland Plateau, just west of the Sequatchie Valley escarpment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palmer%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Tennessee State Route 108 passes through Palmer, connecting it with Gruetli-Laager to the west and Whitwell to the southeast.",
